1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:03,160
(Transcribed by TurboScribe. Go Unlimited to remove this message.) Apple runs a 3 billion parameter language model

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:04,340
right on your iPhone.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:08,240
That's about 3 billion numbers, called parameters, sitting

4
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:09,060
in the phone's memory.

5
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:12,840
The model summarizes text, pulls out key facts,

6
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:14,420
and sorts things into categories.

7
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:16,760
It does all of that while sharing the

8
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:19,100
phone with the operating system and whatever apps

9
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:19,660
you've got open.

10
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,440
And somehow the phone doesn't slow down.

11
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:24,800
It doesn't get hot, the battery still makes

12
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:25,560
it pass lunch.

13
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:28,340
So how did Apple do it?

14
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:31,420
It's a pile of small, careful decisions.

15
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:33,100
And I want to walk through them one

16
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,560
by one, in plain words.

17
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:37,320
If you build apps with AI, they're worth

18
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:39,480
knowing, because they show how to think when

19
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:40,720
you don't have much to work with.

20
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:42,680
Let's start with the problem.

21
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:45,860
A language model reads text as tokens.

22
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:48,180
A token might be a whole word, a

23
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:49,920
piece of a word, or just a punctuation

24
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:50,220
mark.

25
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:53,140
Tokens go in, the model's answer comes back

26
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:54,060
out as new tokens.

27
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:56,320
And in between, they pass through a lot

28
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:56,860
of layers.

29
00:00:57,720 --> 00:00:59,500
Two things make this hard on a phone.

30
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:01,360
The first is space.

31
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:05,060
At 16-bit precision, 3 billion parameters take

32
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:06,300
up almost 6 gigabytes.

33
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:08,280
And that's before anything else.

34
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:10,860
The model also needs room for your prompt,

35
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,200
its calculations, and temporary results, while the phone

36
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:16,200
is still running the operating system and your

37
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:17,640
other apps out of the same memory.

38
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:19,980
The model can't have the whole house.

39
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:23,340
The second is speed, or really, how fast

40
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:24,240
data can move.

41
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:27,240
When the model writes text, the processor keeps

42
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:29,740
pulling numbers out of memory, again and again.

43
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:32,240
Even a very fast chip ends up waiting

44
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:32,620
on them.

45
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:34,920
It's like a lightning-fast chef with a

46
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:36,900
slow helper bringing the ingredients.

47
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:38,940
The chef spends most of the day standing

48
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:39,300
around.

49
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:41,820
On top of that, the phone has to

50
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:44,500
save battery, stay cool, and keep every other

51
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:45,320
app responsive.

52
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:48,400
Nobody wants an AI feature that freezes the

53
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:49,000
whole phone.

54
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:51,180
So those are the two walls.

55
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:53,560
How much memory you have, and how fast

56
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:54,860
you can move data through it.

57
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:57,180
Every trick from here on goes after one

58
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:57,440
of them.

59
00:01:58,060 --> 00:01:58,500
Trick one.

60
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,460
Make every number smaller.

61
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:03,100
The most direct way to shrink a model

62
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,200
is to use fewer bits for each weight.

63
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:07,139
That's called quantization.

64
00:02:07,919 --> 00:02:10,620
Picture a box of a thousand crayons, each

65
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:13,100
one a slightly different shade, next to a

66
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:14,340
box of just 16.

67
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,780
You lose some subtle differences, but the drawing

68
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:18,660
still looks great.

69
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,540
The hard part is finding the balance, where

70
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:23,260
you save a lot of memory and the

71
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:24,480
quality stays good.

72
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:28,480
Apple uses a kind of quantization called palettization.

73
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:31,080
The name comes from a painter's palette, but

74
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:32,980
paint-by-number is an easier way to

75
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:33,620
understand it.

76
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:36,060
In a paint-by-number picture, you don't

77
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:37,820
write the colour name on every spot.

78
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,220
You write a small number, like one, two,

79
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:40,780
or three.

80
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:42,680
And a key at the bottom says which

81
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:43,900
paint each number means.

82
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:46,580
Palettization does the same thing with weights.

83
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:48,620
A lot of weights have nearly the same

84
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:50,100
value, so you put them in groups.

85
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:53,080
A small lookup table holds one example value

86
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:55,200
per group, and that table is the key.

87
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:57,760
Each weight then stores just a short note

88
00:02:57,760 --> 00:02:59,420
that points to a spot in the table.

89
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:01,480
How short can that note be?

90
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:03,980
A bit is a zero or a one.

91
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:06,260
So two bits give you four different notes,

92
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:07,760
enough to point to one of four table

93
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:08,100
entries.

94
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:09,820
Four bits give you 16.

95
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:12,500
Compared to a normal 16-bit number, a

96
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:14,560
two-bit note is eight times smaller.

97
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:17,120
In an earlier version of the model, Apple

98
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:18,980
mixed two-bit and four-bit notes.

99
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:21,000
So some weights got two bits, and others

100
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:21,500
got four.

101
00:03:21,720 --> 00:03:23,960
On average, that came to 3.7 bits

102
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:24,440
per weight.

103
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:25,920
Let's run the numbers.

104
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:28,720
3 billion weights at 3.7 bits each

105
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:30,900
is about 11.1 billion bits.

106
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:33,200
Divide by 8 to get bytes, and you

107
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:35,120
land at roughly 1.39 gigabytes.

108
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:37,720
Compare that to the almost 6 gigabytes we

109
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:38,980
had at 16 bits.

110
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:40,380
It's less than a quarter.

111
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:42,660
So why mix sizes at all?

112
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:44,760
Because not every part of a model is

113
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:45,600
equally sensitive.

114
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:47,240
It's like packing a box.

115
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,700
Socks can get squished flat, but a glass

116
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:51,240
vase needs a lot of bubble wrap.

117
00:03:51,980 --> 00:03:53,520
Some parts of the model are socks.

118
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:55,320
Apple squeezes those harder.

119
00:03:55,740 --> 00:03:57,780
Others are vases, and they get more bits.

120
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:00,380
The newer model goes further.

121
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:03,000
The main decoder weights are down to two

122
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,440
bits each, and the embedding table uses four

123
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:05,980
bits each.

124
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:09,040
The decoder is the main body of the

125
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:11,000
model, the part that does the thinking and

126
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,160
writes the answer one token at a time.

127
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:15,820
The embedding table works like a dictionary.

128
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:17,820
You look up a token, and it gives

129
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:19,180
you back a list of numbers the model

130
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:19,740
can use.

131
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:21,320
There's a catch, though.

132
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:24,200
Rounding every number to a handful of choices

133
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:25,400
is a big change.

134
00:04:25,700 --> 00:04:27,360
If you do it after the model has

135
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:30,060
finished learning, the small errors pile up and

136
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:30,980
the answers get worse.

137
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:34,960
So Apple uses something called quantization-aware training.

138
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,440
Instead of squeezing the model at the end,

139
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:39,560
you squeeze it while it's still learning.

140
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:41,780
From the start, the model works with the

141
00:04:41,780 --> 00:04:43,780
small set of values it'll actually use.

142
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:46,380
It attempts its task, sees where it goes

143
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:48,680
wrong, and its weights get nudged to fix

144
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:51,140
those mistakes, with the squeezing rules still in

145
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:51,460
place.

146
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:54,020
That loop repeats all through training.

147
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:56,420
It's like a drawing test where you only

148
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:57,420
get four crayons.

149
00:04:57,820 --> 00:04:59,860
If you practice with a thousand and switch

150
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:02,020
on test day, your drawing comes out messy.

151
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:04,320
If you practice with four the whole time,

152
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:06,060
you learn how to make a good picture

153
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:06,600
anyway.

154
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:09,980
For developers, that means you shouldn't shrink a

155
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:12,040
finished model and just hope it holds up.

156
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:14,440
Train it knowing it's going to be shrunk.

157
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:15,280
Trick two.

158
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:17,460
Don't do the same homework twice.

159
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:20,300
Small weights are only half of the memory

160
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:20,680
story.

161
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:23,240
The model needs memory while it works, too.

162
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:26,440
Say it's read a paragraph and started answering.

163
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:28,960
To guess the next token, it needs information

164
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:30,220
from all the earlier ones.

165
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:33,560
If it redid every calculation from scratch each

166
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:35,300
time, it would waste a huge amount of

167
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:35,580
work.

168
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:38,620
So it keeps a KV cache, short for

169
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:39,720
key value cache.

170
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:41,900
It's a notebook where the model writes down

171
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:43,860
results it already worked out, so it can

172
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:45,920
look them up next time instead of recalculating.

173
00:05:46,440 --> 00:05:49,140
But the notebook costs memory, and it gets

174
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:50,720
bigger as the text gets longer.

175
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:53,480
A model's weights can fit just fine, and

176
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:54,800
it can still run into trouble on a

177
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:57,140
long document because the notebook gets too fat.

178
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,300
So now you've got two targets.

179
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:01,860
Shrink the weights, and shrink the cache.

180
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:05,220
For the cache, Apple uses cross-layer KV

181
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:05,740
sharing.

182
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:08,020
Instead of every layer keeping its own notebooks,

183
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:09,380
some layers share one.

184
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,080
Imagine the model as a building with eight

185
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:12,520
floors.

186
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,020
The bottom five each keep their own notes,

187
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:17,480
the top three keep none and just read

188
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:18,720
the notes from floor five.

189
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:21,360
That's three notebooks gone out of eight, and

190
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:23,760
three over eight is 0.375, so the

191
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:26,480
cache uses 37.5% less memory.

192
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:29,220
The lesson for developers is to not stop

193
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:30,700
at the weights when you plan memory.

194
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:33,200
With long inputs, the cache matters just as

195
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:33,500
much.

196
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:39,960
Compression makes one model smaller, but a phone

197
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:41,200
needs a lot of different features.

198
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:45,060
One writes short notification summaries, another rewrites long

199
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:47,280
text in a different style, another pulls out

200
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:47,900
key facts.

201
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:50,120
The lazy way is to build a separate

202
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:51,200
model for every job.

203
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:53,280
But they'd all share the same basic language

204
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:55,180
skills, so you'd be storing the same knowledge

205
00:06:55,180 --> 00:06:55,900
over and over.

206
00:06:56,620 --> 00:06:57,700
Adapters fix that.

207
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:00,000
An adapter is a small set of extra

208
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,280
parameters that changes how the base model behaves

209
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:03,120
for one task.

210
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:05,100
The base model is the actor, and the

211
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:06,000
adapter is the costume.

212
00:07:06,460 --> 00:07:07,740
Same actor, different hats.

213
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:10,580
Adapters are built with a method called LoRa,

214
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:12,760
short for low-rank adaptation.

215
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:15,320
You freeze the base model so its weights

216
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:17,540
never change, add a few small pieces on

217
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,560
top and train only those pieces for the

218
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:20,060
new task.

219
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:23,280
That's far cheaper than training or shipping a

220
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:23,940
whole new model.

221
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:26,560
Each of Apple's adapters is only tens of

222
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:28,520
megabytes, which is a tiny fraction of the

223
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:28,940
base model.

224
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:31,720
On the phone, one base model stays in

225
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:33,660
memory, and the small adapters get swapped in

226
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:34,940
and out depending on the task.

227
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:40,020
Even a small model needs a plan for

228
00:07:40,020 --> 00:07:40,560
the hardware.

229
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:43,000
An iPhone has several kinds of processors.

230
00:07:43,620 --> 00:07:45,980
The CPU does general purpose work.

231
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:48,940
The GPU handles lots of number operations at

232
00:07:48,940 --> 00:07:49,640
the same time.

233
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:53,100
The Apple Neural Engine runs machine learning work

234
00:07:53,100 --> 00:07:53,600
efficiently.

235
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:55,900
But you can't just point the model at

236
00:07:55,900 --> 00:07:57,140
one of them and call it done.

237
00:07:57,620 --> 00:07:59,080
You still have to pick the right data

238
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:01,740
layouts, split big arrays into pieces that are

239
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:04,480
easy to handle, and avoid extra memory copies.

240
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:07,140
It's a lot like moving boxes between rooms.

241
00:08:07,740 --> 00:08:09,620
Carry one at a time and copy each

242
00:08:09,620 --> 00:08:11,480
one along the way, and it takes forever.

243
00:08:12,020 --> 00:08:14,060
Pack well and carry smart, and it goes

244
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:14,420
fast.

245
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:17,860
Those small choices change both speed and memory

246
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:18,240
use.

247
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:19,480
Trick five.

248
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:21,100
Know when to ask for help.

249
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:23,360
Even with all of that, the model on

250
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:24,220
the phone has limits.

251
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:25,140
And that's fine.

252
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:27,820
The small model is good at summarizing, pulling

253
00:08:27,820 --> 00:08:30,340
out information, and sorting things into categories.

254
00:08:30,900 --> 00:08:33,520
When a request comes in, Apple Intelligence first

255
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:35,220
checks whether the phone can handle it.

256
00:08:35,659 --> 00:08:37,520
If the job is harder, the request can

257
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:39,140
go to private cloud compute.

258
00:08:39,820 --> 00:08:42,200
Private cloud compute is a protected space in

259
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:42,580
the cloud.

260
00:08:43,140 --> 00:08:45,780
Only authorized software can process your data there,

261
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:47,940
and outside parties are blocked from seeing it.

262
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:51,140
Your device encrypts the request for verified servers

263
00:08:51,140 --> 00:08:53,280
and sends it over an encrypted connection.

264
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:56,160
Any machine outside that boundary doesn't have the

265
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:56,860
keys to read it.

266
00:08:57,400 --> 00:08:59,380
The goal is that even Apple's own staff

267
00:08:59,380 --> 00:09:01,340
and admin systems can't see what's in your

268
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:01,740
request.

269
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:05,060
Once it arrives, a separate, larger server model

270
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:05,700
takes over.

271
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:09,000
It uses something called mixture of experts computation,

272
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:11,260
along with parallel processing tracks.

273
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:13,920
Put simply, a big model can pick the

274
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:16,140
best parts of itself for each job, spread

275
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:17,000
the work around.

276
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:19,000
So the system has two gears.

277
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:20,680
Do it on the phone when you can,

278
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:22,240
and call the cloud when you have to.

279
00:09:23,140 --> 00:09:25,060
Now, what's new in 2026?

280
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:27,500
Apple keeps updating these models.

281
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:31,240
This year it launched AFM3 Core, a new

282
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:34,220
generation of the 3 billion parameter model, along

283
00:09:34,220 --> 00:09:36,320
with AFM3 Core Advanced.

284
00:09:37,300 --> 00:09:39,100
Core Advanced is a sparse model.

285
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:40,320
What does that mean?

286
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:43,780
In a normal or dense model, every parameter

287
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:45,020
works on every token.

288
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:47,620
A sparse model is split into lots of

289
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:50,940
smaller specialist parts called experts, and for each

290
00:09:50,940 --> 00:09:52,700
job it wakes up only the ones it

291
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:53,060
needs.

292
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:54,900
The rest just sit idle.

293
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:57,460
It's like a big hospital with hundreds of

294
00:09:57,460 --> 00:09:57,780
doctors.

295
00:09:58,160 --> 00:09:59,580
You only ever see the two or three

296
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:00,500
who match your problem.

297
00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:04,720
Core Advanced has 20 billion parameters in total,

298
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:06,860
but only one to four billion are active

299
00:10:06,860 --> 00:10:08,540
at a time, depending on what the Apple

300
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:09,820
Silicon system can handle.

301
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:11,580
So you get the knowledge of a big

302
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:13,380
model at roughly the running cost of a

303
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:13,820
small one.

304
00:10:14,420 --> 00:10:16,200
The storage side is clever too.

305
00:10:16,680 --> 00:10:18,980
The full model lives on flash storage, and

306
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:21,140
only the experts your prompt needs get loaded

307
00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:21,740
into memory.

308
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:24,720
The system doesn't swap experts in from flash

309
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:25,840
for every single token.

310
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:28,500
Think of a huge library in the basement,

311
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:30,480
with just a few books you need sitting

312
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:31,020
on your desk.

313
00:10:31,660 --> 00:10:33,360
The cloud side grew as well.

314
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:36,180
Private cloud compute workloads can now run on

315
00:10:36,180 --> 00:10:38,480
NVIDIA GPUs in Google Cloud.

316
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:41,540
Zoom out and there's one idea underneath all

317
00:10:41,540 --> 00:10:41,840
of this.

318
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:44,400
The model's power should match the resources it

319
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:45,440
has at runtime.

320
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:48,480
A small phone gets a small squeezed model.

321
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:50,980
A stronger device can wake up more experts.

322
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:53,160
A hard request can go to the cloud.

323
00:10:53,480 --> 00:10:56,280
No single model has to fit every situation,

324
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:57,700
so the system adapts.

325
00:10:58,520 --> 00:10:59,920
So what can you take from this for

326
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:00,640
your own work?

327
00:11:01,080 --> 00:11:02,480
Here are six things I'd remember.

328
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:04,360
Know your two walls.

329
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:07,560
Memory size and memory speed are separate problems,

330
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:08,740
and you need to deal with both.

331
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:10,520
Compress carefully.

332
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:12,920
Spend more bits where the model is sensitive

333
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:15,280
and fewer where it's tough, and train with

334
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:16,420
the compression in mind.

335
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:18,800
Watch the cache, not just the weights.

336
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:21,500
Long inputs can fill up memory even when

337
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:22,340
the model is small.

338
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:25,060
Share the base and swap the hats.

339
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:28,100
Adapters let many features live on one foundation.

340
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:30,940
Match your code to the hardware.

341
00:11:31,620 --> 00:11:34,580
Layouts, chunking, and fewer copies matter nearly as

342
00:11:34,580 --> 00:11:35,640
much as the chip itself.

343
00:11:36,140 --> 00:11:37,540
And plan a fallback.

344
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:39,180
Run locally first.

345
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:41,120
Go to the cloud only when the job

346
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:41,660
needs it.

347
00:11:42,100 --> 00:11:43,700
And protect the data on the way there.

348
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:46,700
To wrap up, Apple didn't get a language

349
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:49,120
model onto an iPhone by finding one big

350
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:49,580
secret.

351
00:11:50,140 --> 00:11:52,560
It got there by spotting waste and cutting

352
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:54,200
it out, one piece at a time.

353
00:11:54,780 --> 00:11:55,460
Smaller weights.

354
00:11:55,900 --> 00:11:56,660
A slimmer cache.

355
00:11:57,100 --> 00:11:58,800
A shared base with light adapters.

356
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:00,280
Code built for the hardware.

357
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:02,600
And a safe path to the cloud for

358
00:12:02,600 --> 00:12:03,580
when the phone needs help.

359
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:07,220
If you're building AI for limited devices, that's

360
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:07,700
the takeaway.

361
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:10,520
Before you ask for a bigger device, go

362
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:11,720
looking for what you're wasting.
